---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import InflationLab from "../../components/InflationLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What Happened to Your Dollar?"
  description="Real US price data, rebased to any year: watch inflation split apart — college and healthcare soaring while toys and clothing fall — and see how much buying power your dollar has lost."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>What happened to your dollar?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      "Inflation" gets reported as one number, but that average hides wildly
      different stories. Pick a year, and watch how the real price of everyday
      things pulled apart — college and healthcare climbing far past the average,
      while toys and clothing barely moved or got <em>cheaper</em>. It's data most
      people never see, drawn straight from the government's own price indexes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <InflationLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why one number isn't enough</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The basket is an average.</strong> The headline CPI blends
          thousands of prices. Two households with the same income can feel very
          different inflation depending on whether they're paying tuition and
          medical bills or buying electronics and clothes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Services rose; many goods fell.</strong> Labor-heavy services —
          education, healthcare, childcare — tend to climb fastest, because
          productivity gains are hard there. Manufactured goods — TVs, toys,
          clothing — got cheaper as global production and technology improved.
          Economists call the first half of that the <em>Baumol effect</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your personal inflation is your own basket.</strong> What
          matters for you is how prices moved for the things <em>you</em> actually
          buy — which is why the category mix, not the headline, drives whether
          your raises keep up.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is why investing to outpace inflation matters: cash quietly loses the
        buying power you see erode here, while owning productive assets is how you
        try to stay ahead of it. Educational only, not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="inflation" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/inflation" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }
  .ss-headline-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-headline-value {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ss-headline-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  .ss-stats > div {
    background: var(--color-surface-alt);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .ss-stats dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .ss-stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
